From c195d4eed821d7808ec616812f42a714826cbc03 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 11 Jul 2026 10:28:34 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 06795/11362] Record wa30 L2 route-computation dead-end (v5
 probe)

v5 shifted from improvised live play to explicit computation: extracted
exact grid coordinates for the L2 puzzle and exhaustively searched all
block-order x bay-cell assignments for the minimum movement cost. The
optimistic lower bound (80 movement actions) exceeds the 64-cell timer
by 16 even before steering overhead -- a proven infeasibility bound
under a plain Manhattan-distance cost model, not another budget-out
near-miss like v3/v4. Logged as a registry dead-end so a future attempt
starts by testing the cost-model assumption (multi-carry, push, or a
non-Manhattan tile cost) instead of re-optimizing the same route.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JntPrUGLNesPcJH1pyfFYw
---
 ops/arc_solve_registry.yaml                   | 39 ++++++++++++++++++-
 .../outer_loop_gpt56sol_wa30_probe_v5.json    | 11 ++++++
 2 files changed, 49 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
 create mode 100644 results/outer_loop_gpt56sol_wa30_probe_v5.json

diff --git a/ops/arc_solve_registry.yaml b/ops/arc_solve_registry.yaml
index 3365ac62d2..b7fffcc4b3 100644
--- a/ops/arc_solve_registry.yaml
+++ b/ops/arc_solve_registry.yaml
@@ -807,7 +807,44 @@ games:
   levels_reproduced: 1
   win_condition: first-solve L1; ACTION + kind sequence replays offline.
   solver: results/experiment_4275 solve_trace.actions.
-  gotchas: []
+  gotchas:
+  - 'L2 mechanic (outer-loop probes v1-v5, 2026-07-11, gpt-5.6-sol via codex, not yet
+    adaptered): a 64-cell orange bar is a MOVEMENT timer that depletes on ACTION1-4
+    (not on ACTION5 pickup/drop). Five outlined blocks must each be carried and
+    explicitly dropped into one of five distinct target slots; a temporary overlap
+    is not a placement, only frame.levels_completed advancing is ground truth; all
+    five are required (four confirmed insufficient, v3/v4).'
+  - 'Carry-while-repositioning (holding a block against the target while moving
+    before the explicit drop) does NOT save timer budget vs. drop-then-repick (v4
+    refuted this efficiency hypothesis empirically).'
+  - 'Voluntary mid-level resets on L2 risk eventually clearing the L1 checkpoint;
+    timer-forced-expiry resets preserve it safely — never trigger a reset yourself
+    on L2.'
+  dead_ends:
+  - 'v1-v2 (results/outer_loop_gpt56sol_wa30_probe.json, _v2.json): improvised
+    step-by-step play, discovered the L2 mechanics above but ran out of step
+    budget before a full 5-placement route.'
+  - 'v3 (results/outer_loop_gpt56sol_wa30_probe_v3.json): 4/5 placements in one
+    continuous run, 197/250 steps used, timer expired before the 5th.'
+  - 'v4 (results/outer_loop_gpt56sol_wa30_probe_v4.json): 4/5 placements again,
+    296/350 steps used, independently confirmed the same 4-placement ceiling and
+    refuted the carry-while-repositioning efficiency hypothesis.'
+  - 'v5 (results/outer_loop_gpt56sol_wa30_probe_v5.json): switched from improvised
+    play to explicit computation — extracted exact grid coordinates (player,
+    5 blocks, 2x3 six-cell receiving bay) from the post-L1-prefix L2 frame, then
+    exhaustively searched all 120 block orders x every 5-of-6 bay-cell assignment
+    for the minimum movement-only cost (ACTION5 charged as free). Optimistic
+    lower bound = 80 movement actions, vs. the 64-cell budget — infeasible by 16
+    even before steering/orientation overhead, under a plain Manhattan-distance
+    movement-cost model. This is a proven bound, not a budget-exhaustion result:
+    if the naive one-block-at-a-time carry model is complete and correct, this
+    level cannot be won in the timer as currently understood. Two live
+    possibilities remain unexplored: (a) an undiscovered mechanic reduces true
+    cost below 64 (multi-carry, a push-not-carry option, a shortcut/warp), or
+    (b) the level genuinely requires a different tile-cost model than plain
+    Manhattan distance (e.g. diagonal movement, or ACTION1-4 do turn-then-step
+    rather than a pure translate). Next attempt should probe (a)/(b) directly
+    rather than re-running route optimization under the same assumed cost model.'
 - game: s5i5
   reproducibility: reproduced
   levels_reproduced: 2
diff --git a/results/outer_loop_gpt56sol_wa30_probe_v5.json b/results/outer_loop_gpt56sol_wa30_probe_v5.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..3afccd6b6b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/results/outer_loop_gpt56sol_wa30_probe_v5.json
@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+{
+  "game": "wa30",
+  "starting_level": 1,
+  "max_level_reached": 1,
+  "total_env_steps_used": 33,
+  "reproduction_verified": false,
+  "action_sequence": [],
+  "computed_min_cost_route_length": 80,
+  "narrative": "After the 33-action level-1 prefix, the rendered 16x16 movement lattice showed the solid carrier at (6,9), its direction cursor at (3,2), outlined blocks at (10,5), (12,6), (9,7), (12,8), and (11,10), and the 2x3 receiving bay at x=3..4, y=7..9. An offline exhaustive search over all 120 block orders and every distinct assignment of five blocks to five of the six bay cells found an optimistic minimum of 80 movement actions, with ACTION5 pickup/drop charged as zero; this exceeds the 64-cell budget by 16 before any steering-inertia overhead. Per the required decision rule I stopped without executing that infeasible route, so levels_completed remained 1 and there was no winning sequence to submit to reproduce().",
+  "solve_provenance": "live_agent_self_discovery"
+}
